<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/20050824_flowers.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/20050824_flowers.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/20050824_eggs.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/20050824_eggs.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/20050824_plants.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/20050824_plants.jpg"></a>
<p>
We then went to check out our first temple.  Tin Hau Temple.  For some reason, this seems to be a popular temple name as there are 3 of them we ran across throughout the day with the same name.  However, this one was one of the oldest in Honk Kong.  The process for praying involves taking a bundle of incense sticks (they are quite large and long) and burning them simultaneously over a flame.  Then you raise them up and down while you say your prayer. Afterwards, you take a few and place them in a pot full of sand in front of each god/goddess.  On the ceiling hangs these enormous spiral incenses and all are burning.  Signs are around saying to watch out for falling ash.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1125334050_4.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1125334050_4.jpg"></a>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1125334050_4.jpg>-->
<p>
Following Tin Hau Temple on Kowloon Island, M and I went over to the Jade Market.  This place is by far the cheapest place to buy jade jewelry in Hong Kong. We should know as we asked at several shops around HK.  They expect you to bargain and that we did and found we got a great deal.
<p>
We happened to stumble to another market now on our maps located near Jade Market.  It was full of fruits, vegetables, seafood, and meats.  It was an amazing display of colors, shapes, and sizes.  We ended trying a fruit which I've never seen back home. It's called Dragon Fruit.  We had asked the shopkeeper what it was called but she didn't speak English and my lonely planet guidebook wasn't took helpful in how to ask her.  We happened to walk into a grocery later on in the day and say it labelled there.  Anyway, it's a pink/purple fruit with a white meaty inside and little black seeds, and it's absolutely delicious.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1125333947_2.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1125333947_2.jpg"></a>
