<p>
It was time to head for the MTR to Hong Kong Island where the Man Po Temple is located. This temple was being reconstructed but was the most popular.  A small line to go in and so much incense burning at once, the smoke from the incense was burning my eyes.  On the way there, it rained hard.  Luckily, M and I had our umbrellas, but it only helped a little.  My shoes were wet as well as parts of my shirts/pants.  I hate walking around all day with wet socks.  I also found out that my backpack isn't waterproof. Luckily the electronics were ok, but the Lonely Planet book got waterlogged. Still readable and usable but the the pages are moist and stained and stick together. Oh well.  
<p>
We needed a break from the rain and decided to go into a tea shop which served some of the best Chinese tea I've ever had. They were herbal teas capable of relieving thurst, headaches, and many other sicknesses.  But best of all, they were tasty.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1125333947_3.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1125333947_3.jpg"></a>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1125333947_3.jpg>-->
<p> 
The rain had mostly died down, so we decided to catch a train to the Stanley Market and Repulse Bay. This is where I have to fault the city of Hong Kong's maps and also the Lonely Planet guide.  We had no idea how to catch the bus to Stanley, and went the wrong way, before we finally asked a train cashier.  We had to go back 6 stops to Central, and walk to the bus terminus.  But we found it and took a double decker bus. We were fortunate to sit in the front row on the front of the bus and got a great view of the beaches and the Ocean on the way to Stanley.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1125334050_2.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1125334050_2.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1125333947_1.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1125333947_1.jpg"></a>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1125334050_2.jpg>
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1125333947_1.jpg>-->
<p>
Stanley Market is known to be the cheapest place in HK to buy souvenirs.  It certainly was true.  I bought a few things but would have liked more if I could only carry it with me.  Repulse Bay is a touristy Westernized area. It's for Americans who want to feel like they are home away from home.  I really hope people staying there visit the real HK.  It's also one of the only places to accept credit cards. Most of HK only takes cash.
<p>
M and I decided to have Happy Hour drinks in a bar called Wildfire located in Murry House.  Murry House is the first colonial style house in Hong Kong.  HK doesn't have a stellar history with preserving historical buildings. Murry House was originally moved from it's old location to Stanley because they needed the room for a bank or apartment hi-rise.  They decided that instead of tearing it down, they would move it piece by piece to the new location. They, numbered each brick so they could reconstruct it later. However, they managed to lose a few pieces. They did turn up later, but the numbers on the bricks wore off due to the humidity and so during the reconstruction, they managed to displace 6 pillars.  The 6 pillars are standing outside on their own in front of Murray House.
<p>
M and I ended up wrapping the night up with a walk through Ladies Market and Ya Shen Market. Ladies Market mostly sells womens items but Ya Shen is where you get sporting goods.
<p>
Stayed tuned for more tomorrow ....  
